@@ -0,0 +1,226 @@
+//! A count per phase: what one run of a query cost, split by the part of the engine that paid it.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.15-budget-regression`. `jobq/3.22-bench-budget` argued that a budget measured in
+//! milliseconds is a budget the machine decides, and that the counts a loop left behind are the
+//! same numbers in every process. `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` applied that to this store: one
+//! number, `pruning_rate`, says how much of a store a query did not have to open. Neither answers
+//! the question a budget that went red actually asks — *which part of the query moved?* — and this
+//! module is that answer: one counter per phase of a run, in the value the run reports.
+//!
+//! # A phase is where the work happened
+//!
+//! Seven phases, in the order a query walks them, and every charge is a count of something the
+//! engine really did — never a number derived afterwards from another one, because a number derived
+//! afterwards cannot say which phase it came from.
+//!
+//! | phase | charged once per |
+//! | --- | --------------- |
+//! | [`Phase::Headers`] | segment the plan examined: every entry of the manifest it was made from, whether the plan opened it, pruned it by the range or pruned it by the index |
+//! | [`Phase::Blocks`] | segment file whose block was read |
+//! | [`Phase::Decompress`] | block inflated |
+//! | [`Phase::Decode`] | record decoded out of a block that was read |
+//! | [`Phase::Filter`] | record the predicates *kept*: the metric `FROM` named and every `WHERE` pair |
+//! | [`Phase::Aggregate`] | value the window pass folded into a window |
+//! | [`Phase::Render`] | bucket written into an answer |
+//!
+//! The phases are a *pipeline*, and the shape of each one is a decision worth stating:
+//!
+//! * the **plan** is the only phase a query pays for whether or not it reads anything. Pruning is
+//!   cheap and not free (`lakelet/4.11-query-engine`), and a query that opens nothing still read
+//!   every segment's stated span to find that out;
+//! * **reading** is two phases because a file is read and then inflated: a block whose seal does not
+//!   verify is a block that was read and never inflated, which is why the pair is `>=` and not `==`;
+//! * **decoding** is charged on the way in, before any predicate is asked, so a query whose
+//!   predicates match nothing still paid for the records it read;
+//! * **filtering** is the predicate and *not* the range. A record the predicates kept can still be
+//!   outside the query's range — that is what the first and last window of a range that is not a
+//!   whole number of windows long are made of (`lakelet/4.10-downsampling`) — and the range is the
+//!   window pass's question;
+//! * **aggregating** is what the window pass took in: a value that became part of a window. A
+//!   `GROUP BY …, series` query hands the pass the same records once per series
+//!   (`lakelet/4.11-query-engine` pays a scan per series), so counting what was *offered* rather
+//!   than what was *folded in* would count the same record several times and break the identity
+//!   below;
+//! * **rendering** is the answer's own shape: one per window that held a record. A window that held
+//!   nothing is not a bucket.
+//!
+//! # The identities are what make it a measurement
+//!
+//! A counter no case can falsify is worse than no counter: it is a number a reader trusts and a
+//! defect does not move. So the phases are not seven independent tallies — each one is a subset or a
+//! superset of another, and the spec pins the pair on real runs:
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! Headers   >= Blocks >= Decompress        every read block was decided about, and read before it was inflated
+//! Decode    >= Filter >= Aggregate >= ...  every decoded record is offered to the predicates, every kept one to the window pass
+//! Aggregate >= Render                      a bucket holds at least one value
+//! ```
+//!
+//! and one identity ties the trace to the counters `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` already reports:
+//! every segment the plan examined is **opened**, **pruned by the range** or **pruned by the
+//! index**, so `Headers` is those three added up — the conservation rule the pruning counters are
+//! written in, now said about the phase that did the deciding.
+//!
+//! # A share is whole percent of the work that ran
+//!
+//! [`Trace::shares`] answers the other half of the report: not *how much* each phase cost but what
+//! part of the run it was. Whole percent, largest first, only the phases that ran, and the rounding
+//! the floors leave goes to the largest remainders: a profile that added up to 99 is a profile
+//! somebody has to explain, and a share of a run that did nothing at all is not a number
+//! (`lakelet/4.15-budget-regression` finds nothing to divide by and answers no shares rather than
+//! seven zeroes).
+//!
+//! # Two runs, one report
+//!
+//! [`Trace::merge`] adds one run's counts to another's, which is what a caller who ran two queries
+//! needs in order to answer one question about the pair. The counts add, and so do the runs behind
+//! them ([`Trace::runs`]), because a report that has lost count of how many runs it covers is a
+//! report whose total nobody can check against anything.
+
+/// How a phase's counter is found in a [`Trace`]: the phase's own place in [`Phase::ALL`].
+///
+/// Every count a trace holds is at the position its phase has in the list below, so the list is the
+/// one place the phases are enumerated and a reordering that broke the correspondence would break
+/// the spec rather than a reader's profile.
+const PHASES: usize = Phase::ALL.len();
+
+/// One phase of a run: the part of the engine a count belongs to.
+///
+/// The order is the order a query walks them, and it is load-bearing: [`Phase::ALL`] is that order,
+/// a profile is printed in it, and the trace's counters are stored in it.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash)]
+pub enum Phase {
+    /// The plan: one per segment whose stated facts were examined.
+    Headers,
+    /// The read: one per segment file whose block was read.
+    Blocks,
+    /// The inflation: one per block taken out of its compressed form.
+    Decompress,
+    /// The decode: one per record read out of a block.
+    Decode,
+    /// The predicate: one per record the metric and the `WHERE` pairs kept.
+    Filter,
+    /// The window pass: one per value folded into a window.
+    Aggregate,
+    /// The answer: one per bucket written into it.
+    Render,
+}
+
+impl Phase {
+    /// Every phase, in the order a query walks them.
+    ///
+    /// The one list: a profile is printed from it, a trace counts in it, and the spec pins what it
+    /// holds. A phase that is not in it is a phase nothing reports.
+    pub const ALL: [Self; 7] = [
+        Self::Headers,
+        Self::Blocks,
+        Self::Decompress,
+        Self::Decode,
+        Self::Filter,
+        Self::Aggregate,
+        Self::Render,
+    ];
+
+    /// The phase's name: the lowercase word a profile's line and the answer's document spell it
+    /// with.
+    ///
+    /// `headers`, `blocks`, `decompress`, `decode`, `filter`, `aggregate`, `render` — the names the
+    /// course's baseline (`course/tracks/lakelet/budget/4.15-budget-regression.toml`) is written
+    /// in, which is why they are asserted rather than described.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn name(self) -> &'static str {
+        todo!()
+    }
+
+    /// The phase `name` names, or nothing when no phase is called that.
+    ///
+    /// The inverse of [`Phase::name`], for the reader of a profile: a script that has a
+    /// `lakelet-budget decode=60` line has a name and needs the phase it belongs to.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn parse(name: &str) -> Option<Self> {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.15-budget-regression: the phase `{name}` names, or nothing")
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one run of a query cost, counted phase by phase.
+///
+/// A value: built empty, charged as the run happens, compared, summed with another run's, and read
+/// out as a profile. It holds nothing but counts — no time, no instant, no name of a machine — which
+/// is what makes two runs of the same query over the same store equal.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Trace {
+    /// One counter per phase, in [`Phase::ALL`]'s order.
+    counts: [u64; PHASES],
+    /// How many runs this trace is the sum of: one for a trace a single query charged, and as many
+    /// as were merged into it.
+    runs: u64,
+}
+
+impl Trace {
+    /// A trace that has counted nothing: one run, no work.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new() -> Self {
+        todo!()
+    }
+
+    /// Charge `count` of `phase`'s work to this run.
+    ///
+    /// Called where the work happens: a plan counts the segments it examined, a decode counts the
+    /// records it read, a window pass counts the values it folded in. A count of zero is nothing to
+    /// charge and does not make a phase have run.
+    pub fn charge(&mut self, phase: Phase, count: u64) {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.15-budget-regression: {count} of the {phase:?} phase's work, added to this \
+             run"
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// What this run was charged for `phase`.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn count(&self, phase: Phase) -> u64 {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.15-budget-regression: the {phase:?} phase's counter")
+    }
+
+    /// Every phase's count, added up: the whole of what the run did.
+    ///
+    /// The denominator of [`Trace::shares`], and the one number that says a profile is empty.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn total(&self) -> u64 {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.15-budget-regression: every phase's counter, added up")
+    }
+
+    /// The phases that ran, as whole percent of the total, largest first.
+    ///
+    /// Whole percent of a run that did nothing is not a number, so an empty trace answers an empty
+    /// list rather than seven zeroes. The percents add up to exactly 100: whole percent of a whole
+    /// run leaves a remainder, and the phases with the largest ones take it, a point each, because a
+    /// profile that adds up to 99 is a profile somebody has to explain.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn shares(&self) -> Vec<(Phase, u64)> {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.15-budget-regression: the phases that ran, as whole percent, largest first"
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// Add another run's counts to this one: two runs, one report.
+    ///
+    /// The counts add, and the runs behind them add with them ([`Trace::runs`]). A caller that has
+    /// answered two questions and wants one account of the pair starts from the first run's trace
+    /// and merges the second into it.
+    pub fn merge(&mut self, other: &Trace) {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.15-budget-regression: {other:?}, added to this run: two runs, one report")
+    }
+
+    /// How many runs this trace is the sum of.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn runs(&self) -> u64 {
+        todo!()
+    }
+}
+
+impl Default for Trace {
+    /// A trace that has counted nothing, which is what a caller starts from.
+    fn default() -> Self {
+        Self::new()
+    }
+}
