@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+//! What one run of a query cost, counted phase by phase.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.15-budget-regression`. `jobq/3.22-bench-budget` taught the shape of a budget a
+//! slow machine cannot fail — a counter, a guard over its growth, and the names a gate compares —
+//! and `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` made one cost of this store a number (`pruning_rate`). What
+//! neither of them answers is the question a red budget actually asks: *which part of the query
+//! moved?* This module is that answer, as a value: a count per phase, in the module that owns the
+//! counting rules.
+//!
+//! The counting happens where the work does — `crate::engine::query::exec` charges a trace as it
+//! walks a plan — and the rules about what each phase means, the identities between them and what a
+//! profile is made of live in [`counters`], because a count whose meaning is decided at the call
+//! site is a count two call sites can disagree about.
+//!
+//! A trace is *reported*, never *stored*: nothing in this store keeps a profile of a past run, and
+//! nothing here reads a clock. A count of what the engine did is the same number on every machine,
+//! which is the property `jobq/3.22-bench-budget` argues for and the reason this step measures work
+//! rather than time.
+
+// The modules a later step adds are declared inside this block: a scaffold may only write inside a
+// `kiln:scaffold:begin`/`kiln:scaffold:end` region, which is what keeps your own edits safe
+// (docs/authoring.md §5).
+// kiln:scaffold:begin
+// `lakelet/4.15-budget-regression`: the counter, the phases it is counted in, and the identities
+// that make the pair a measurement.
+pub mod counters;
+// kiln:scaffold:end
+
+pub use counters::{Phase, Trace};
